    "result_text": "All tests pass and the build succeeds.\n\n## Summary\n\nI implemented address normalization for owner creation in this Spring Boot app, following the codebase's established normalizer pattern.\n\n**New component** \u2014 `rest/controller/AddressNormalizer.java` (a peer to the existing `EmailNormalizer` and `TelephoneNormalizer`). Its single responsibility is to canonicalize an address: strip, collapse internal whitespace to single spaces, upper-case, and expand whole-word street-type abbreviations (`ST`\u2192`STREET`, `RD`\u2192`ROAD`, `AVE`\u2192`AVENUE`). It returns `\"\"` for null/blank input. Expansion is token-based, so it won't mangle words like `STATION`.\n\n**Wiring** \u2014 `OwnerRestControllerV1.addOwner`: I inject the normalizer and normalize the address on the incoming `OwnerFieldsDto` as the first step, before `ownerFieldsValidator.validate(...)`. This single placement satisfies every requirement cleanly:\n- The **required-field check** now sees the normalized value, so an address that is blank after normalization (e.g. `\"   \"`) becomes `\"\"` and is rejected with 400.\n- The mapped `Owner` carries the normalized address, so it is **stored and returned** normalized.\n- **Household duplicate detection** (`findOwnersInSameHousehold`) and the **shared household id** (`joinHousehold` \u2192 `HouseholdIdGenerator`) already read `owner.getAddress()`, which is now the normalized form \u2014 and since every created owner is stored normalized, all comparisons happen on consistent, normalized data. No changes to `ClinicServiceImpl` were needed.\n\nI left `updateOwner` untouched, since the spec scopes normalization to creation.\n\n**Tests** \u2014 the full suite is green: `Tests run: 3, Failures: 0, Errors: 0` (the three new acceptance tests covering whitespace/case, abbreviation expansion, and blank-after-normalize rejection), `BUILD SUCCESS`.",
